\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 Chapter 9 \endash  Gone Rogue
\par 
\par It was night by the time I began to make my way back to Malachi\rquote s apartment. Dark, bu
t cloudless, the moon nearly full in the sky and throwing the streets into stark relief between patches of pitch and muted blue-gray. The sky was polluted with lantern light here, the stars hardly visible, but I could vaguely pick out the rearing head of 
the Ciraberos constellation, one of the most prominent in the sky regardless of how clear your vista was. Sailors used it vociferously, swearing by the eye star. Even above their compasses, sometimes. I\rquote d once read that when sailing very far north
, compasses could become unreliable. I wasn\rquote t sure if that was true, but the Admiral who\rquote d written about it certainly seemed to think so.
\par That\rquote s how I felt right now. Throughout the whole of my life, even before I\rquote d been taken in by the Abbey, God had been my north star. My guiding light, and the assurance of
 hope for all I had ever wanted to be, or could be. But right now, I felt untethered, sailing into unknown waters, drifting farther and farther away with each passing day from His guidance and the clarity of mind that the conviction of my truth 
gave me. I had never felt so alone\'85 so deep in the bramble that His Word was audibly fading.
\par It wasn\rquote t as though I\rquote d ever have claimed to be in communication with God, directly. I was no prophet. But growing up, Father Helstrom had taught me how to hear my \lquote inner voice\rquote , a beacon inside your
 heart, inherent to all of God\rquote s intelligent beings. Feral animals lacked an inner voice, which is why they lived shameless lives of depravity and what we would consider \lquote sin\rquote . But it wasn\rquote t their fault\'85 they didn\rquote 
t know any better. They were made to be hunted, shepherded, and to carry on the natural processes of the world. They were lesser beasts. 
\par But we had the gift of morality. The inner voice of God, directing us in our lives. We could choose to ignore it of course, and many did. We might suffer the bite of humiliation and guilt for doing so, to various degrees. Some were better at ignoring thos
e punishments, as well. Those people were shameless, guiltless, and more prone to sin because of it. It wasn\rquote t inherent. You couldn\rquote t be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2051107 born}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  that way.
\par But you could wear down your ability to hear your inner voice by ignoring it, by not grappling with your guilt, and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2051107 not giving Confession.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 And I was worried\endash  nay, scared\endash  that I had done just that. 
\par Throughout my frantic, obsessive pursuit of the Heart Thief, I had on many occasions ignored those quiet warnings. Those hesitations, my uncertainty when I\rquote d engaged in something suspect\'85 my inability to fully grapple with my sin. I\rquote 
d begun repressing it, pushing it aside until some later date, convincing myself that what I was doing in that present moment was more important.
\par It was so easy. So easy, I realized, to destroy yourself this way while still }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2051107 believing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  you\rquote 
d had the best intentions. And still, even though I was coming to realize this, even though I was unable to lie to myself any more about the fact that I was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2051107 slipping}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 , and I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2051107 knew}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  it\'85 
\par \'85I couldn\rquote t stop myself.
\par Because the fact is, I didn\rquote t go straight back to Malachi\rquote s apartment. Didn\rquote t pack up my things, and turn right back around and march directly to the Ministry headquarters. I didn\rquote t follow orders, that night.
\par I found myself, instead, prayer book gripped in one paw as I walked, in the moneyed neighborhood the Pedigree estates occupied. This would be considered \lquote high town\rquote 
 in most cities, but here in Redcoven it was not so very separate from the trade district. Only a few roads over, the paving stones just slightly newer and less cracked. Red, like the clay in the rivers here, as the city\rquote 
s name suggested. Painted with icy swirls of hard-packed snow in between the patterns they\rquote d been laid out in. A marvel of canine engineering, really. To think how much clay had to have been extracted, how many bricks made, and laid out. The }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2717330 work}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  that went into building our cities was mind-boggling, when you thought about it.
\par And it was all done by us common people, in service for our entire lives to those of us who were better bred, and thus closer to God. Graced and blessed from birth. If we served well in our lives, we\rquote d be born better than our current life. We
\rquote d grow just a rung closer to the ideals these people were already living.
\par And here I was, hunting one of them. Stalking him, really, like a predatory Dyre. Hoping he\rquote d slip up. Hoping he\rquote d give me something\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2717330 anything}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 . Some advantage I could use.
\par Of course, he had to know he was under watch. Perhaps I wasn\rquote t the man he was looking out for, but he knew of my existence, knew the Ministry well from his last investigation. He doubtless knew how to hide his misdeeds.
\par I don\rquote t know why I thought I\rquote d learn anything that night, walking the back alleys behind the Estate Apartments. I just felt I had to try. Because I was desperate.
\par I\rquote d very quickly found the address of the apartment Cillian Rathborne would be taking up residence in while he stayed in Redcoven, and of course passed it on to Malachi. It wasn\rquote t hard. He didn\rquote 
t own property within the city limits under his own name, but his connections were easy enough to trace. Other than the Lane Estate, there was a low-bred Pedigree woman- a spinster- who lived within the city limits in her family\rquote 
s apartment. She had inherited it young and had never moved, living here on a permanent basis with a few servants and little else. Ellen Olman was her name, if memory served. She was a long-time family friend of the Rathbornes
 apparently, noted in the Archives for having attended most of their events and gathers, and keeping up a small cotton milling business with their assistance. 
\par She was probably a personal friend of Cillian\rquote s, and it hadn\rquote t taken long for Malachi to confirm that he
 was staying with her here. We had yet to receive clearance to search the premises, but Malachi had demanded compliance from Cillian at the door once already and been given free reign of the place. It wasn\rquote t quite as thorough as a search would be
\'85 in that we couldn\rquote t damage any of their property to get at areas we couldn\rquote t otherwise\'85 but even Malachi seemed to think they weren\rquote t harboring him there. The place was too obvious; as I said, they }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid14616659 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  to know we were watching it.
\par There were Ministry agents at every office in every city that weren\rquote t Templar, and blended in better with the population. It was possible one of the servants I saw outside sporadically along these service roads behind the 
estates was one of them. If they were, I probably wouldn\rquote t know. It was also possible one of them might have recognized me, but I was keeping my scarf and my cloak hood up over my face.
\par Hardly suspicious. It was cold out.
\par It begged the question }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid9066214 why}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  I didn\rquote 
t want to be recognized by my own Ministry, though. Which was another of those misgivings I was currently ignoring.
\par I found a spot tucked out of sight, behind a frozen mound of wood mulch left over from the gardeners, and between the back stone wall of an abutting yard. I swept my cloak
 underneath me and sat, tucking myself up as much as possible against the cold, my breath in the air the only giveaway of presence to anyone passing on the street.
\par I sat, and waited, for hours. I\rquote m not certain how long. And because it was there, because I had it back, I read through my prayer book. Returning again and again to the paragraph-long letter he had written me in between the psalms of forgiveness
 and my own journal scribblings.
\par I dissected every word, every interpretation of what they could possibly mean, over and over again. Trying to find the sarcasm, malice, or some hidden accusation he\rquote 
d intended. But every attempt to twist the words, to make them mean something other than what they said, felt dishonest. And pointless.
\par I couldn\rquote t square what the Heart Thief had done with\'85 the person, the very real person, I was beginning to know. Something was off, something was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid3345148 wrong}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 , and I could not hold on as well to that thread of conviction I had left. Not as strongly as I had when it had been a rope, firmly tethering me to the righteous fury I had felt towards hi
m at the outset.
\par I felt in my bones that there was a deeper truth, a deeper understanding to be had here. And was that not my inner voice as well? Father Helstrom had always said it could be faint, could be hard to hear above the noise
 the secular world created, but that when we heard it, we would know. We would know in our heart what was right and wrong.
\par And this had all begun to feel\'85 wrong.
\par I was more confused than I had ever thought I could be, when considering the life of a thief and murderer. And it wasn\rquote t even as though I doubted that they\rquote d }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid3345148 
done}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  the crime. That wasn\rquote t where my hesitation lay, exactly.
\par I just needed to know why. I needed to understand the Heart Thief, or I\rquote d go mad, I was certain. This disorganized state of my mind- where I was beginning to fear I could not tell right from wrong anymore-
 was entirely due to this case. And more importantly, due to Darcy himself.
\par I didn\rquote t actually think he\rquote d coming walking out that back door. But as the early evening wore on, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid3345148 someone}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  did. Someone I didn\rquote t recognize.
\par The man was a fox with a black coat and silver tail-tip, and a dash of silver on his face. He looked older, not ancient but a bit grey at the temples. He was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid18968 certainly}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  no servant, dressed as well as he was, but he was using the servant exit\'85 and made sure to check the street first with a flick of his ears and a bob of his slender muzzle back and forth.

\par Then he took the stairs down and began to walk the service road. North. He carried a black, bulging leather bag in one hand, but was otherwise unburdened. No weapons of any sort that were visible, nothing to suggest he was dangerous.
\par At worst if I was found out, I could feign innocence and claim that crossing paths was a coincidence. 
\par I followed him.
\par I shadowed him from a distance, easily able to pick out his dark silhouette against the still snow-covered city, even at night. When he t
urned a corner I sped up, stopped and slowed back down a pace once I had him back in sight again. He was walking at a normal pace and not taking unnecessarily abrupt turns, likely just taking the shortest route to wherever he was going. If he realized he 
was being followed, he didn\rquote t let on.
\par It was a long walk through the city, until at last, I saw him from the edge of a four-way street, stride up to, unlock and enter a residence. We were in the trade district, in mid-town. Solicitors, legal clerks, and\'85
\par Physicians.
\par I kept my normal walking pace as I moved along the street towards, and then passed by the house he\rquote d entered, not pausing except to glance at it. Long enough to read the sign.
\par Ruun Mandrake, Physician and Barber Surgeon.
\par 
\par 
\par I made it back to the Archives at last, late into the night. Malachi\rquote s lanterns in the apartment upstairs were not lit, save one small bloom of diffused orange that may have been a candle.
\par I bypassed the stairs and headed for the downstairs door to the Archives, pulling out a set of keys in shivering hands and wincing at the protesting groan of the lock and the subsequent squawk of wood as I opened the door. I\rquote 
d really rather not have had to explain all of this to Malachi just yet, let alone in the middle of the night.
\par I shut the door as quietly as I could, shucked off my hood and clenched and unclenched my hands, willing sensation back into them. Usually, my fur and pads were enough to protect me from the cold. I only wore gloves when I rode. But I\rquote 
d been outside a long time.
\par I coughed a few times to alleviate the burning in my throat, but did so into my palm to remain as quiet as possible. Lord, I was acting like a criminal now, I really was. But\'85
\par I found the first box of archives easily, because although we locked everything down when we weren\rquote t here, I\rquote d come
 for this box innumerable times already. The main key to the Archive ledgers opened it, being as the man in question was currently under investigation. It was an open file.
\par I sat, skimming the ledgers quickly because of how well I knew them by now, until I found the documents I was looking for. Cillian Rathborne\rquote s medical history.
\par Born with an atrophy of the legs and spine, which had ultimately afflicted every other child born to his parents\rquote  line. Cillian was the only to survive childhood, and it was belie
ved for years that he would not walk. He had surpassed expectations there, growing into a passably healthy young man, save the continued weakness in his legs and his reliance on a cane. He saw a Physician in Amurfolk for continuing care for his condition
\'85
\par But there, there it was. The name I\rquote d hoped, expected even, to find. When he was a child, he\rquote d spend the spring and summers here in Redcoven, with the Lanes. And he\rquote d see a Physician here\'85 a Doctor Mandrake.
\par We\rquote d had no reason to investigate or even take note of the man, at least not yet. We were running down a list we\rquote d compiled of all of Cillian\rquote s contacts, but the amount of people who\rquote 
d crossed paths with him or had connections to his family throughout the files on him was\'85 massive. He was a Pedigree, he had associates, servants, tradesmen who\rquote 
d worked for his family a-plenty. Tracking down and questioning all of those who lived in the city would be an enormous task. 
\par But this man, at least, I knew had visited with him. He\rquote d been acting suspicious. He\rquote d checked to see if he was being spied upon- what possible reason could he have for that, save illicit activity? There was the possibility he\rquote d
 only been visiting with Cillian for a physical exam, or to prescribe something, but-
\par I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid9514244 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  to look into him. Seizing onto the first real bolt of certainty I\rquote 
d felt all day, I stood and crossed the room towards the back stacks, tucked into a closet with no door. It didn\rquote 
t need one, this place was sealed behind multiple locks on the reinforced door, the windows, and every box in the Archive was locked, as well. Different levels of permission to access them. Different keys.
\par The black books for civilians of Redcoven: financial documents, deeds, taxes, birth certificates and their histories with the judicial system, were authorized to be accessed only by Ministry agents from }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid11558363 this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  city, or Inquisitors themselves. Malachi had only given me the general key.
\par I glanced up to the ceiling, and twisted my ears, listening for the man. He must have been home, but sleeping. He wouldn\rquote t be out at this hour, and he knew I intended to head for the Ministry and Saint Rhine\rquote 
s today. I doubt he was worried about me enough to be out looking for me. He wasn\rquote t my minder, after all. 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid13061875 \'93I hope you never have a use for this. But in pursuit of the truth, sometimes man\rquote s law must come secondary.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 When Father Helstrom had said that to me, long ago, I doubt he had meant }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid13061875 this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 . But, wincing through every motion at this point was becoming a tiresome farce. I was going to do this, and I knew it. Best to just get on with it.
\par I reached up to the shoulder joint of my harness and worked a claw beneath the strap to find the thin metal implements that were looped into the cloth
 on the underside. They were meant to be accessible with one hand if need be, and were sized perfectly for my somewhat clumsy fingers. They could poke into my chest sometimes when I extended my arm upwards, so I\rquote d removed the
m and put them in my pack for the last few years, essentially disregarded. A misjudgment on my part, because if I\rquote d had them when Darcy had locked me in that room\'85
\par They stayed on me within hand\rquote s-reach at all times, now. I would not make that mistake twice.
\par It had been a while since I\rquote d done this, but I\rquote d actually perfected the method Father Helstrom had taught me\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid13061875 on}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  these sorts of locks, in our own far less protected Archives room. I was certain these locks in a real Archive room were more complex, that the mechanism would be altogether different than the ones I\rquote 
d managed to trip in the past, or that God himself would strike me down for my hubris here\'85
\par But I found the familiar mechanism, fiddled the implement into place after a scant few wrong guesses, and then it just\'85 opened. Like it always had before, when I\rquote d practiced.
\par I stared at the box in dumb shock. For some reason, I\rquote d really thought this would be harder. But I suppose they relied on their exterior security and the Inquisitor who took up residence here to keep these records safe.
\par I opened the box to reveal the black books, sorted alphabetically. It didn\rquote t take long for me to find the right one. There, at my fingertips, was this man\rquote 
s entire life. Collected in one small section of one book, stored in one footlocker among hundreds. The Ministry had files like this on nearly every citizen of 
Amuresca, whether lowly or high-born. Expressly for moments like this, so that men like us could crack open their lives as surely as you might a book, and read about their sins and successes.
\par You\rquote re told about this kind of power and privilege, especially being raised as I was. It\rquote s portrayed as aspirational. It\rquote s what we train for. I\rquote 
d never really considered it as anything but inevitable and expected. Experiencing it in the moment was\'85 very different to what I\rquote d anticipated, though. It felt intrusive, and made me self-aware in a way I\rquote d not expected.
\par They had these files on all of us. On Malachi. On me.
\par Eventually, when I confessed all of this to whomever would hear me, this would be in my file. What I was doing right here, right now. And someone would read it someday, and judge me for it. Perhaps rightly.
\par I leafed through the volume, until I reached Ruun Mandrake\rquote s file. No pictures, but the history, profession, gender, and species was correct. Had to be him. I tried to avoid the unnecessary sections, looking for the obvious, first. 
\par Crime record- none reported. 
\par Incidents with heresy- none reported. 
\par No notations of moral corruption, either. He attended church, irregularly. Wife, deceased, children were grown now by the look of their birth dates. Son had gone into practice on his own, daughter had married.
\par All profoundly average. Not a whiff of anything untoward, and no brushes with even civil law. His taxes were paid, and-
\par I paused, tracing a claw tip to the small notations made yearly by the Archivist who kept track of property taxes. He had two residences listed, but there was an empty block halfway down one column, back nearly fifteen years. A property he\rquote 
d stopped paying taxes on, listed on the first year he\rquote d stopped paying not as \lquote unpaid\rquote  but \lquote condemned\rquote . 
\par I narrowed my gaze at it, reading back a page to where the residence first entered the log. He\rquote d inherited it from his own father, and it was listed as \lquote The Lodge at Dyre Point\rquote .
\par A lodge. A hunting lodge, likely, given how rural it was. Dyre Point was so afar field from the roads that it wasn\rquote t listed on most maps. I only knew of its existence because its name had stood out in my mind when I was looking over Malachi\rquote 
s local maps. It was ten miles or so from here, in the Dyre Wood. Dyre Point was a rocky, mountainous area towards the northernmost end of the woodlands. Other than hunting, it was inhospitable country with little to offer.
\par Foxes were fond of hunting, and those that could afford the land and permits to do so often kept lodges in their family. This one must have been abandoned for financial reasons or just not kept up well by the man who owned it, becaus
e it had been given up to the wilderness fifteen years ago.
\par I clenched my teeth inside my muzzle. If ever there were a safehouse to harbor a criminal\'85 that would be it. Ten miles.
\par Ten miles was reachable in a snow storm. On horseback\'85 maybe even on foot. Especially if they knew the way.
\par It was a hunch, and a thin one. But I\rquote d followed my instincts in the past and they\rquote d yet to lead me astray. 
\par A thud from over my head startled me so much, I fumbled and dropped the book, wincing as it fell, cockeyed on its sid
e. The spine seemed intact though, so I gingerly picked it back up and set it in the box. Then pushed the box back into place, and re-manipulated the lock.
\par The noise from the second floor had been singular, not rhythmic like footsteps, and too quiet to have been something falling. It more sounded like something dropping in a controlled motion.
\par There was a heavy creak, and the faintest sound of shuffling. It was a body, I realized. Someone lying or sitting on the floor. They were still doing so.
\par It had to be Malachi, but why was he on the floor? Oh- knees. Of course. He was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid6957372 praying}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 . 
\par \'85 or he\rquote 
d fallen out of bed. Either way, he was awake. Which meant I needed to hasten up to the apartment, lest I be caught in the act. There was a staircase from his small sitting room right into the Archives, and it would not take him but a moment to lea
ve his bedroom, cross the small space, and open that door.
\par I made sure to leave the Archives Office first, locking up quietly, before ascending the staircase into the apartment from the outside. I was so far beyond recognizing how guilty I was acting, by now\'85 it was becoming second nature.
\par I switched to the apartment key, working it into the lock, when I heard another more startling sound.
\par A distant, muffled clap, followed by an unmistakable groan of pain.
\par Oh God, he was hurt. He must have fallen? Or he\rquote d been hurt during the day, in some altercation I didn\rquote t know of yet. 
\par I shoved open the door, calling his name out immediately into the quiet darkness of the apartment.
\par \'93Malachi?!\'94 I closed the door quickly, turning the lock behind me and hustling down the small hallway towards where I\rquote d heard the noise. \'93Malachi, are you hur-\'93
\par The smell of blood knocked me back a step. But after the brief stagger, I rushed on through the sitting room towards the closed bedroom door, trying the knob immediately and finding it blessedly unlocked.
\par \'93Isidor,\'94 his voice stopped me when I had the door open an inch. The smell of blood was stronger now, and incense\'85 the cloying, cedar kind we used to cover odors in the Abbey. 
\par His voice sounded reedy, threadbare. Certainly pained. But oddly serene. He didn\rquote t sound angry, at least. I tried pushing the door open more slowly, and he spoke again.
\par \'93Don\rquote t enter if you are squeamish, young man,\'94 he murmured. \'93My penance isn\rquote t for the faint of heart.\'94
\par I nodded as though he could see me, and steeled myself for\'85 I knew not what. But the sight that greeted me would not have been in my wildest imaginings.
\par He was nude, for one. And that might not have alarmed me in and of itself, even though we took an oath of modesty at sixteen. I\rquote d grown up around other boys and share
d a dorm with them as they became young men. And Malachi and I shared a somewhat similar build; he was only a bit leaner and taller.
\par But it was the injuries\'85 the spattered splashes of blood across the wooden floor, and the weapon in his hand that made my throat close up.
\par He was kneeling in prayer, as I\rquote d guessed. He had his Tome open towards the latter third of the Arynthioch Chapters on Redemption, if my relative estimation at how deep he was into the book was sound.
\par He had a\'85 whip\'85 shorter than a horse whip, but branched into several separate tendrils of wrapped, glistening leather, lying limply-splayed on the floor, near the worst of the bloodstains. 
\par Well, no, really the worst of the bloodstains were on his }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid6689728 back}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 . Broken crimson lines between his dark brown
 fur, more obvious when they crossed into the lighter variations of his pelt down his inner arms, midsection, and rear.
\par What\rquote s more, there were }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid6689728 so many others there}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
, aged and healed-over, from what must have been years of overlapping abuse of his flesh
. Many had disappeared into his fur, but in some place the scar tissue was so dense, the fur could not grow any more. His back was a map of prolonged, ongoing torture. Some of it crossed over his shoulders, down his clavicle and neck, some on his arms,
 legs and buttocks. Even the arch of his tail.
\par I had no words. When he slowly turned to regard me, he only stared at me evenly across that small room, and offered nothing but exhausted serenity. He looked like the monks did when they sang in the choir for 
hours, or fasted for days. Tired. Broken. But ready.
\par Ready to hear The Word.
\par I knew it must have been some form of self-flagellation for prayer. The monks would engage in rituals of arduous labor when they fasted sometimes, or sit in ice water baths, or steaming tents, to cleanse themselves on holy days. But this was\'85
\par \'93You\rquote ve come back very late,\'94 Malachi said. I only nodded, and for whatever reason, he seemed to accept that, and gestured beside him on the floor. \'93Come. Sit.\'94
\par I could do nothing else. I removed my cloak and laid it over the chair at his desk, then stepped up beside him and slowly kneeled at his side, crossing my wrists in front of me and clutching one knee. I murmured a qu
iet prayer of my own, just my evening recitation. He listened, swaying slightly beside me, and joined me by the last few sentences when he presumably recognized the prayer.
\par It felt surreal, this moment. I noted his Tome just long enough to confirm I\rquote d been right about what prayers he was reading. Redemption and cleansing the sinful soul. The story of Adele, the wife of one of the prophets, who\rquote 
d flung herself into the sea to appease God\rquote s wrath and put an end to the storm that was ravaging their city. In doing so, she had redeemed herself in His eyes for her transgressions against her husband, and died cleansed of her sin.
\par To be honest, it was a passage I avoided. Historians had long since unearthed alternative tellings of the story of Lady Adele, in holy texts written by monks nonetheless, that involved her being tried and sentenced 
for her crimes against her husband, and that made her less of a willing martyr and more just\'85 a woman who\rquote 
d been caught and punished for having an affair. The sacrifice was meant to be noble, but the truth of it was muddy, and the account was not written by the Prophet in question himself. It was\'85 dubious. Enough that I had questions.
\par This close to Malachi, the smell of blood absolutely filled my nostrils. I\rquote m certain he saw my nose twitch, the wince in my eyes, and he was not afraid to point it out. \'93I know I\rquote m a sight,\'94 he said quietly. \'93Does this\'85
 frighten you?\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 I answered honestly. \'93I was just\'85 startled.\'94
\par \'93It is a spiritual discipline,\'94 he said. \'93The ritual of mortification of the flesh. It is how I make penance for a lifetime of misdeeds, Isidor. It releases me, for a time, from the burdens I carry. The weight of them.\'94
\par I simply nodded. I wanted to say more, I just couldn\rquote t find the words. I understood this. Deeply. There were many times\'85 including quite recently\'85 when pain or discomfort had helped alleviate the wro
ngness I felt in my soul. Even something as simple as doing heavy labor, or pushing myself to train an additional hour, caused pain. And that pain could be cleansing.
\par \'93I\rquote ve never seen this method,\'94 I said at a near-whisper. 
\par \'93It is only,\'94 he said, turning to regard me with a snap of his piercing gaze, \'93for the most severe treatment of a relentlessly sinful mind. Don\rquote t you get any ideas; you are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid13971536 young}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  yet, and whatever sin is inside you is still malleable. There are many forms of penance and re-education you can pursue before this becomes\'85
 necessary.\'94
\par I swallowed thickly, staring into my lap. I could still feel his gaze on me when I asked, \'93What\'85 Brother Malachi\'85 could you possibly think is so sinful in you to warrant such brutal treatment of your own flesh?\'94
\par \'93That is between me and God,\'94 he said, his voice weak. \'93But you must trust in my judgment and the judgment of my handlers, Brother Isidor. It }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid6689728 is}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  warranted.\'94
\par I looked across our laps, from my own hands to his. One was fastened to his own knee, the other hanging limply at his side, still holding the wrapped leather handle of the odd whip, sticky with blood.
\par \'93Can I help you to bed, Brother?\'94 I asked at last. \'93Are your prayers\'85 complete?\'94
\par \'93You will not be joining me tonight, I take it?\'94 he asked. \'93I spoke to your Brother Dolus. He informed me you\rquote d be moving your residence to the Ministry, with him.\'94
\par I considered how to answer that. I\rquote d shared this apartment for several nights now with Malachi, and he\rquote d been very decent and hospitable, even giving me the one bed to sleep in, while he\rquote 
d taken the nearby sitting chair. But I was not about to allow him to sleep there again tonight\'85 not with several fresh wounds on his back. I had been enough of an imposition as it was.
\par \'93I\rquote m staying here tonight,\'94 I assured him quietly. \'93It\rquote s late, and you need tending to, and you\rquote ve been so kind as to do so for me, for days now. One more night is fine.\'94
\par He was looking at me, his eyes searching, and there was more than pain there. There was\'85 a vulnerability. An open question, but what it was, I could not say. Maybe it really was just the pain. Maybe I was imagining things.
\par \'93I\rquote m sorry, by the way, if Brother Dolus was unkind to you,\'94
 I said, when the thought occurred to me. I wrapped an arm gingerly, lower on his back along his hips, and began to help him up. He stood under his own power, only wavering a bit, and probably only from pain. He hadn\rquote t lost enough blood that he
\rquote d be in danger of fainting\'85 I think. I\rquote d have to trust that he knew his own limits.
\par He certainly seemed to have been doing it for a long time, by the look of those scars.
\par He huffed. \'93Remarkably affable, actually. He said nothing that was wrong\'85 only reminded me of what I should have carved onto my heart, long ago.\'94
\par I had no idea what he\rquote d meant by that, but I focused instead on maneuvering him into bed, and then going about getting him some clean smallclothes, and putting a pot of water on the wood-burning stovetop to warm some wash cloths. 
\par \'93They\rquote re shallow,\'94 he assured me. \'93More painful than dangerous. That\rquote s the point. I\rquote ll be fine, lad, don\rquote t worry yourself over much.\'94
\par \'93You took care of me,\'94 I said around a cough. \'93Least I can do.\'94
\par He arched an eyebrow at me from across the room. \'93Sounds like I ought to be, still. Maybe you shouldn\rquote t have been out all day in the cold, young buck. Sounds like you haven\rquote t kicked it just yet.\'94
\par \'93Lie down on your stomach,\'94 I said as I dropped the wash cloths in the pot. \'93And try not to roll over in your sleep tonight. I\rquote ll try to ensure the same.\'94
\par \'93Oh, please don\rquote t sleep in that chair,\'94 he muttered. \'93Murder on the back. And you\rquote re sick still, you need to stay warm.\'94
\par \'93We can share the bed,\'94 I said. \'93That\rquote s the obvious solution.\'94
\par There was a long pause. And then in a halting voice that was so unlike him it baffled me, he said, \'93We shouldn\rquote t- no. I\rquote m not one of your Brothers, and I\rquote m certain you\rquote ve taken your vow of modesty by now-\'93
\par \'93We\rquote re both men, Malachi,\'94 I said, staring down intently at the pot and not at him. \'93And we\rquote re all Brothers. It\rquote s fine. I\rquote m not concerned.\'94
\par Another achingly long pause. And then, after a quiet sigh. \'93Alright. If it doesn\rquote t concern you, it doesn\rquote t concern me.\'94
\par 
\par 
\par I never really slept. I lay there, my mind spinning, constantly running over the things I\rquote d have to gather, the short preparations I could make, before the morning came. And when Malachi\rquote 
s breathing evened out to deep, slow drags, and I knew he\rquote d fallen  under fully\'85 I made my move.
\par I left the warmth of our shared bed and the distinctive faded scents of cedar, blood, and the diluted alcohol I\rquote d used to clean his wounds earlier. Stepping out into the cold apartment and gathering my possessions, I wondered if he wa
s feigning sleep. If he could truly hear me, and was allowing me the grace of slipping away. If he did, he did not move, or try to stop me. Of course, he thought I was leaving for wholly different reasons. He thought I\rquote 
d be at the Ministry in the morning.
\par When I stepped out into the still dark, frigid air of early morning, and knew without a doubt that I would not take the lane down towards the church
 to the Ministry offices, that I would instead turn right and head down the main street, towards the city gates\'85
\par I knew I was making a decision here that would fundamentally alter the course of my life. Whether I was right or wrong.
\par But the indecision was gone. There may have still been guilt- I could hardly tell beneath the power this compulsion had over me- but I was certain I could do nothing else.
\par The dawn light was muted by incoming clouds, and snow began to fall almost as soon as I made it outside the gates onto the main road. God was either telling me to turn back, or closing the door behind me to any others who would follow.
\par 
\par 
\par White tufts spun in drifts through t
he haze of jagged shadows that dominated my vision. The occasional stagger of my burning, failing legs tipped the pale forest floor into my peripheral, but I tried not to look down. Each inch I let my muzzle drop was one I could not recover from, my head 
drooping steadily towards surrender.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \tab 
My feet were miles behind me, lost in the numbing cold. Not literally, obviously, I had to believe they were still under me, only because I was somehow still upright. The insulation of the fur wraps might save my toes- I couldn\rquote 
t be sure. I was past worrying about such trivialities, had passed that threshold hours ago. At this point if I lived long enough to make it to the Lodge, or }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid742736 any}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  place of respite for that matter, I\rquote d thank God all my days for it. I\rquote d include my gratitude for this mercy in every prayer, morning and night.
\par \tab Even with two separate maps of the countryside and carefully plotting what should have been a day-long hike at most, I had once again failed to account for the truly devastating effects of the weather, and time, on old roads. I hadn\rquote t 
been walking a mile before everything faded to white, blocking my path both forwards and backwards with an indomitable wall of terrifyingly blank forested countryside.
\par \tab There was a sun up there, somewhere, muted behind the drifts and the grey, and it remained my only means of navigation, but even }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid462648 it}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  disappeared beneath the canopy of sunken, swollen branches and the ice throwing fractals of what little light existed in all directions. 
\par \tab My arms were clutched in a cross over my chest, greedily holding what heat somehow blessedly remained in my body to my core. My throat burned all the way down, the frigid air crystallizing l
ike caltrops, lodging somewhere deep below my ribs. As if the cold itself were a personal foe, seeking to exploit the weakness it knew was there.
\par \tab I had not, in fact, fully recovered from the cold I\rquote d been nursing all month. I had over-estimated myself again, it seemed. Brother Dolus would have said I was suffering the trials of my own arrogance, and he would be right to say so. 
\par \tab In my illogical thought maze, I had persistently told myself I must look ahead, and only ahead. Forward. I could still vaguely see the outline of what must have once been a
 road, even with the stifling coat of fresh snow (now nearly up to the top of my shins). I knew- or at least I thought I knew- that I was still traveling in the right direction, because of my occasional glimpses at where the sun must have been.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 But it, too, was aban
doning me. Disappearing below the horizon, which meant I\rquote d been out here trying to walk these ten miles }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid7488098 all day}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 . }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 Hardly surprising- the snow masked the obstructions of jagged rocks, downed branches and heaven knew what else, and when I wasn\rquote t arduously 
and slowly picking my way around them, I was stumbling into or often losing my footing to them, which had resulted in stumbling off-trail, catching my feet painfully in a pinch or jab, or just outright falling. I\rquote 
d even gotten my leg caught up in the spokes of an old, broken wagon wheel once, a metal edge tearing into my calf and drawing blood.
\par These roads were old. Nearly abandoned, save for hunters, criminals, and those who hunted criminals.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \tab And thus they weren\rquote 
t particularly well-maintained.
\par \tab My foot caught on something again; it hardly mattered what but a fallen branch most likely. That was almost always the culprit.
 I barely had the strength to remain upright and moving in the best of circumstances right now, let alone with one foot tripped out from underneath me and the other finding no pu
rchase on the loose, snow-covered dirt. I pitched forward, sucking in a sharply-cold breath on the way down.
\par \tab With so much snow on the ground and my body as swaddled as it was, it should have been an easy fall. But every step hurt my aching, weary limbs at
 this point and I did not tumble with any grace. The pale ground rose up to meet my jaw before my arm could catch beneath it, sending my teeth up in a snap, biting the edge of my own muzzle. My vision went white hot for a moment, and I tasted blood.

\par \tab Then 
one of those ice-cold knives in my chest pricked me particularly sharply, sending a wracking cough through my body that seemed likely to shake me apart. Each subsequent hacking gasp hurt all the worse. My lungs begged for air, but each breath I sucked dow
n was colder than the last. I felt as though I would choke on it, drown in the air as surely as I would in frigid waters.
\par \tab Squeezing my eyes shut, begging between breaths, I rocked to my side and tried vainly to bring my own body under control. But I was faltering. I was losing ground.
\par \tab No. I was dying. Wasn\rquote t I?
\par \tab That\rquote s what this was. It felt too dramatic to think that to yourself, I suppose, until the moment it was true. The dizzying, all-encompassing pain. The primal fear of being unable to draw enough breath, or the right }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid10699983 kind}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  of breath, to satisfy my body\rquote s needs. The odd spikes of energy p
unctuating the deathly weariness. Which one of these would be my literal last gasp?
\par \tab It didn\rquote t feel like I thought it would. There were no memories, no cascade of longing, unrealized hopes going through my mind. And I certainly had many of those. All I could really think on was the pain and the fear.
\par \tab And it was dark. So dark. Getting darker, especially around the edges of my vision. Perhaps I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid10699983 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
 fallen from grace of late, perhaps I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid10699983 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  sinned, but\'85
 where was God? If I was about to die, should I not feel His light? His comfort\'85 or at least his condemnation?
\par \tab Maybe I was farther off from death than I thought. But I was losing my purchase on consciousness, that much I knew. Maybe first I would sleep\'85 and then I would see Him. Maybe He wanted me to rest, now. He couldn\rquote 
t possibly wish for me to die this primordially afraid, this desperate.
\par \tab I was- or at least had been- a faithful servant, my entire life. Although I had stumbled in my path sometimes, I had always sought repentance. That\rquote s why I was here. I had been absolute in my dedication to this task, no matter the risk.
\par \tab Had I not?\tab 
\par \tab I would pray. Truth be told, I had been praying for the last hour, inaudibly or in mutters between my teeth. But perhaps God needed to hear me proclaim, as Malachi had said, how }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid288503 broken}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  I was. How desperately I needed Him right now. Perhaps that\rquote 
s why I suffered. I had not lowered myself before Him. Not fully. Not as I should have. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
I was already prostrate. All that was left was to beg.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \tab 
I could not drag myself to my knees, but I drew them in against my chest, feeling the brush of my tail tucking between my legs beneath the layers of my tunics and cloak. I had lost sensation in it long ago, at least anything beyond numb pain.
\par \tab \'93Progenitor\'85 Father of Canus\'85 He who has come before\'85 all of mortal flesh and blood\'85\'94
\par \tab My voice was paper thin and stuttered between breathy gasps, gagging on the urge to cough. Still, I pressed on.
\par \tab \'93I beg you, as\'85 all who stood to face the horizon a-and\'85 rose\'85 from the feral crawl of o\'85 our\'85 predecessors... This lowly man\'85\'94
\par \tab A crunch of snow amidst the muffled quiescence of the forest. An animal, perhaps. Come to bear witness.
\par \tab \'93\'85 sixth\'85 from the Holiest\'85\'94 I swallowed, thickly, \'93\'85 six lifetimes apart\'85 from rejoining the Blood. This Sixth Son, begs of you\'85\'94
\par \tab Shifting shadows over my closed eyes. Perhaps the wind jostling the weighted branches of the spruce that caged this road in. I could hear them now, yes. I could feel the fresh powder hit my cheeks, shaken loose.
\par \tab \'93Show mercy,\'94 I breathed out, labored and raw. \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid12475695 Show mercy!}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \'94 I repeated, as loudly as I could. I could hardly hear myself over the pounding of blood in my ears. 
\par \tab Tentative, careful footsteps. A demon- that\rquote s what they were, come to feast on me lest God deny me in this moment- prowled at the 
edge of the road. The knowledge that they were there tickled the fur along the back of my neck, pulling my ears upright. My instincts were muddled, buried beneath the pounding fog of pain. But I at least knew I was not alone. Not anymore.
\par \tab Finally.
\par \tab Even if it was a demon, I was suddenly, profanely glad it was here. I would deny it, I would spit in its face, I would reject any final offer\'85 but I was glad it was here. If God would not answer my call, I was manically, hysterically pleased that 
}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid14314333 something}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  had. If not grace, I would at least have defiance in my final moments.
\par \tab \'93I throw myself upon the altar of your judgment!\'94 I rasped, voice thin as a taut wire, liable to snap at any moment. \'93I am your servant\'85 miserably fallible though I am\'85 I am yours. Please\'85\'94
\par \tab 
What was I asking for? That thought- or the lack of one- brought my pleas to a sudden, choked-off stop. I felt a noise, a desperate keening, wring itself out from the back of my throat, barely emerging past my teeth. The prayers, the supplications, had co
me easily enough. But now that it was time to ask for mercy, I\'85 wasn\rquote t certain what mercy for a man like me might be. 
\par \tab Perhaps death }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid8726942 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
 the escape God was granting me. If I died tonight, I would still die a mostly faithful man. I had yet to cross that horizon into unredeemable
 heresy. Every moment I was alive brought me closer, and there was no certainty I could contend with the sin inside of me. It clawed its way out even in sleep. It had persisted in the waking world, and driven me to\'85
\par \tab No. If I could not admit to my sins now, then when? I had made choices. I had made choices that I knew were wrong, and convinced myself somehow that all of this would be worth it in the end. 
That discovering the truth, selfishly, on my own, through lies by omission, ignoring my oaths and orders, and the theft of information, would prove to be just\'85 because I\rquote d }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2886190 wanted}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  it to be.
\par \tab And look at how He had punished me. Gaze, trembling, upon the justice I had sown and reaped. 
\par \tab But the harm, on the whole, was done to myself. At least no others would suffer or die because of me. I had held fast to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid2886190 most}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  of my tenants thus far. I could be buried clean. If I\rquote d been allowed to continue on this path? Who knew. 
\par \tab Maybe this\'85 this storm\'85 this failing of my lungs\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid681811 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  God\rquote s mercy.
\par \tab More footsteps, soft but drawing closer. A definitive shadow now. Despite the storm, moonlight filtered in through the clouds and my eyes were well-adjusted to the dark, so the shift in light was noticeable. They were close.
\par \tab \'93Let come what may,\'94 I rasped out, \'93to this flesh\'85 but you will not\'85 have my soul.\'94
\par \tab An unmistakable, sharp little noise. A humorless laugh. One I remembered well.
\par \tab \'93Knight,\'94 their voice, low and soft as velvet, but piteous.
\par \tab No.
\par \tab \'93I assure you, I want no such thing.\'94
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid7616506 No.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 I must have said the last \lquote no\rquote 
 aloud, because the figure standing over me gave a quiet tut and fell slowly into a crouch beside me. One dark, slender hand reached out, the tip of a barely-retracte
d claw hooking under my chin and tipping my head in their direction, despite any weak protestation my body could summon.
\par \tab \'93Not whom you\rquote d hoped to find?\'94 They asked me, voice impassive and barely beyond a whisper, sparing my burning ears.
\par \tab Despite everythi
ng, despite the pain and desperation and the encroaching blackness, fury lanced through my body, coupled with a final spike of adrenaline. I felt my teeth gritting, my exhausted muscles coiling. Shaking, I lifted myself onto one elbow, my breath coming ou
t in ragged puffs of smoke in the still, cold air. 
\par \tab And that was literally all I could manage.
\par \tab \'93You,\'94 I uttered, \'93are\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid15348759 exactly}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \'85 who I hoped to find.\'94
\par \tab Warm gold-green eyes stared back at me, ensconced in a beautiful, feline face. One long chain earring hung from a cuff on their left side, barely visible under the edge of a fur-lined cloak. Gold, with an inlaid rosy pink gemstone.
 I vaguely remembered it from the Hudson Estate, adorning the Lady of the house more than once.
\par \tab \'93You\rquote ve made it,\'94 they said, blinking long lashes slowly. \'93You\rquote ve found me\'85. Again. Impossibly. Well done. What now?\'94
\par \tab I had no answer, of course. What could I say? I couldn\rquote t even stand any more, let alone affect an arrest.
\par \tab I had found my quarry. They stood before me. The Lodge must have been }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid7812226 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  near\'85 I probably just hadn
\rquote t seen it through the trees yet.
\par \tab But I\rquote d seen my holy vow through to the end. God would know. He saw me now, even if He chose not to answer my pleas.
\par \tab \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid7812226 Why}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 ,\'94 I demanded in a threadbare, fading voice.
\par \tab They tilted their head at me, ears twitching. \'93Why?\'94 They repeated, uncertainly.
\par \tab \'93Why take the Heart of Faith?\'94 I asked, desperately. 
\par \tab \'93Are you asking me?\'94 they said, nonsensically.
\par \tab \'93Yes!\'94 I cried. \'93What value could it possibly hold for a Heretic like you?\'94
\par \tab \'93None whatsoever.\'94
\par \tab Ungodly rage, anger so sharp I could }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid13321971 feel}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  it\'85 or maybe that was just my chest aching from breathing
, it was so hard to tell\'85
 it threatened to bring the blackness. There was nothing left in me for a physical attack, so the hate just ate at me. Chased away all thoughts of God. Replaced them with white hot, overwhelming frustration. Despicably, coiled with
in it was the rotten core of what I\rquote d felt so many nights, now. Relief, that I could lay eyes on them again. Some part of me was just }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid3752222 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  glad that they\rquote d be the last person I ever saw.
\par \tab \'93Return it,\'94 I slurred, \'93or you will suffer God\rquote s wrath\'85\'94
\par \tab \'93I can\rquote t do that,\'94 they said, almost placatingly. Like they wanted me to feel appeased, somehow.
\par \tab I grasped at them and got\'85 something. The edge of their sleeve. The hand near to my chin. They let me. Stared down at me, that pitying look in their eyes the whole while.
\par \tab It made me feel sick.
\par \tab It made me feel\'85
\par 
\par 
\par \tab I dreamed. In between long bouts of nothingness, peaceful and quiet, the dreams came as they always did. Blessedly most were nonsensical, more like flashes. Nothing I could remember well. The library. The smell of summer 
in the monastery graveyard where we used to play.
\par \tab And then, everything sharpened to a point, to one clear, defined memory. Hardly a dream, because it was real. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid11364663 Had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  been real.\tab Brother Dolus\rquote s lash, on my rear. His hand, well-filed claws gripping bluntly but bruising into the loose ruff around my neck. Dragging me, pitching and fighting, down the staircase to the cellar.
\par \tab The eyes of my brothers, watching me from the kitchen above. Braced, all of us, for what we knew was to come.
\par \tab I\rquote d bitten him. I could taste the ink on his fur, on his hands, where I\rquote d snapped at him when he\rquote d first tried to haul me down here. He was not half-hearted any more. This would not be like the other times.
\par \tab He was angry at me now.
\par \tab A pop of fire and a shifting of logs roused me. The sounds of a hearth. My body was yanked back into the present as though I\rquote d been pulled above the waters of the dream, and I sucked in a breath much the same.
\par \tab It was warm. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid12072884 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  was warm. 
\par \tab Nonetheless, the breath left me in a wracking coug
h. My chest, my lungs, felt thawed. But painful still. That sharp tickle clawed at my throat, making each inhale threaten another cough. I tried vainly to stop, to steady my breathing. But the panic came as it had before when I wasn\rquote 
t able to stop. Each cough brought another, fed the itch in my chest, satisfied it yet made it worse.
\par \tab \'93Lord, that doesn\rquote t sound good\'85 try to calm your breaths, if you can,\'94 a dulcet voice lulled, the bed creaking beside me. \'93Easy now.\'94 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 My eyes were still adjusting to the warm candlelit
\'85 room? I was in a room. Wooden walls. A bed. A roaring hearth.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5317124 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 \tab A man sitting beside me. Slender. Feline.
\par \tab My body jumped, as much as it was capable of. I jarred the bed in my sudden motion, trying to lift myself and failing. Everything still }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid788524 ached}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124 , my muscles would not respond to me. And something was wrong with my arms\'85
\par \tab I tugged at my wrists, laid out comfortably at my sides but something tugged back. I could feel the pressure tighten when I tried to move them up. Bound. I was bound with something. Likely to the frame of the bed below.
\par \tab Prisoner. I was a prisoner.
\par \tab The warm, gentle comfort of this place, this serene setting I\rquote d woken to, shattered as reality snapped back into place for me. I\rquote d been pursuing the Heart Thief. I\rquote d intended to check this property
, this hunting lodge at Dyre Point, because of its connection to Cyllian, to his Physician, and I\rquote d decided to do so on my own, without telling a bloody soul where I was going, because\'85
\par \tab Because I wanted the Heart Thief to myself. Because I was terrified of what Malachi would do, were he to actually capture Darcy this time. Because I knew that once he was arrested, I would never see him again unti
l the day of his execution, and that meant we\rquote d never be able to talk, I\rquote d never have my questions answered, I\rquote d never understand-
\par \tab \'93Please\'85\'94 Darcy spoke again, his voice indeed pleading, or at least affecting something believable as a plea. He genuinely seemed to 
lose his train of thought for a moment, muzzle parting and then closing, before settling on what he wanted to say. He was really selling the lie. \'93Please try not to panic. You\rquote re not well, and I don\rquote t really know how to pro
perly care for someone in your condition, let alone if you fight me.\'94
\par \tab His gaze moved to mine, imploringly, and he repeated, quieter, \'93Please.\'94 
\par \tab I breathed in staggered, rough pants, turning my head back and forth swiftly, trying to take in whatever I could. Looking around, the room seemed bare. Stripped, dusty, unused. But someone- my captor, obviously- had cleaned it up recently.
 There was dust in all the corners and on the ancient crown molding, but not in the immediate vicinity of the floor around us. He\rquote d c
leared the bedside tables and a writing desk, made use of the hearth. A bowl and decanter of clear water sat at the table, alongside\'85 I smelled cheese. Salami. The wrapped bundle must have been bread.
\par \tab My stomach growled, betraying me.
\par \tab \'93Well, now,\'94 the feline slowly smiled, making my insides churn. \'93I didn\rquote t expect that. But if you\rquote re feeling well enough, you }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid489132 should}{
