\rquote t have. You\rquote re just\'85 very funny  sometimes, Isidor, and I don\rquote t think you mean to be. Which alas, makes it even funnier. That was cruel of me\'85 here.\'94
\par \tab He held the tea cup close to my muzzle. The warm aroma, herbal and sweetened with honey, wafted into my nostrils and set me to drooling. I wanted it. I wanted it }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid5317124\charrsid4396423 so}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5317124  badly.
\par \tab \'93It will help with the coughing,\'94 he assured me. And then, after a moment spent considering, he brought the cup to his own muzzle and drank one long swallow. His throat bobbed. Then he returned it to my own muzzle, nodding. \'93See
? No poison. Just tea. Oh, and there\rquote s sugar, and honey, because it\rquote s just\'85 outright uncivilized to drink tea without sweetening. I hope you agree.\'94
\par \tab Another cough threatened. I watched the downy fur along his throat bob in another swallow. He\rquote d certainly imbibed it. It was likely safe. 
\par \tab He had a long, slender neck, half-covered under a loosened cravat. The fur looked soft. The cup was still wet where he\rquote d drunk from it, just along the ridge. I came to a sudden decision.
\par \tab Regardless of what his purpose for me was, whatever hidden motivations he may have had, I could do nothing in service of God or the Ministry if I died of dehydration. Stubbornness here was petulant. 
\par \tab Leaning forward, I mouthed at the edge of the cup and let him tip it back for me. And then I slowly drank, allowing the warm, syrupy tea to coat my throat. I breathed through my nose, and steadily drained the whole cup.
\par \tab \'93There, then,\'94 the feline said. His voice was nearly as soothing as the tea. A siren\rquote s call to damnation. I\rquote d hear that voice taunt me in Hell, if I didn\rquote t survive this.
\par \tab \'93How old are you, Sir?\'94 he asked, suddenly.
\par \tab The question caught me off-guard. Leaning back, and with no real reason I could think of to lie, I answered automatically, \'93Nineteen.\'94
\par \tab \'93So young,\'94 he said, breathing out through his nose in a long sigh, for some reason. Then, before I could think of a retort, he lifted his gaze back to mine. The smile that bloomed there, hesitant like an early lily, was genuine eno
ugh that I saw it in his eyes. \'93I\rquote d obviously rather you not possess this\'85 ineffable ability you seem to have at tracking me down. Earnestly, it\rquote s become exhausting. But in this case, if you were going to throw yourself into the 
maw of the wilderness with reckless abandon anyway, I\rquote m rather glad you found me. If you\rquote d collapsed just half a mile down the road, I would not have heard you from the Lodge.\'94
\par \tab \'93You heard me?\'94 I asked, mortified.
\par \tab \'93Oh, Young Sir,\'94 he looked on me, sadly. \'93You were screaming.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid5060763\charrsid5317124 
